
class Solution {
    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int left = 0;                     // Start of search space
        int right = nums.length - 1;      // End of search space

        while (left < right) {            // Binary search loop

            // Calculate middle index (avoids overflow)
            int mid = right + (left - right) / 2;

            // Make sure mid is even
            // Because pairs start at even indices before the single element
            if (mid % 2 == 1) {
                mid--;                   // Shift to previous even index
            }

            // Check if current pair is valid
            if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) {
                // Pair is correct → single element is on the RIGHT side
                left = mid + 2;
            } else {
                // Pair is broken → single element is at mid or LEFT side
                right = mid;
            }
        }

        // When left == right, we've found the single element
        return nums[left];
    }
}
🧠 Intuition (important!)

Before the single element → pairs follow pattern:

index: 0 1 | 2 3 | 4 5
       a a   b b   c c

After the single element → pattern shifts:

index: ... | 5 6 | 7 8
           d d   e e

So:

If pairing is correct → move right
If pairing is broken → move left
